  "funFacts": [
    {
      "text": "== Description == The adult males grow up to 28–36 millimetres (1.",
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tettigonia_viridissima"
    },
    {
      "text": "The morphology of both sexes is very similar, but the female has an egg-laying organ (ovipositor) that can reach a length of 23–32 millimetres (0.",
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tettigonia_viridissima"
    },
    {
      "text": "Unlike grasshoppers, it is essentially active by day and night, as testified by its endless crepuscular and nocturnal singing.",
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tettigonia_viridissima"
    },
    {
      "text": "== Distribution and habitat == This species can be encountered in most of Europe, in the eastern Palearctic realm, in the Near East, and in North Africa, especially in meadows, grasslands, prairies and occasionally in gardens at an elevation up to 1,800 metres (5,900 ft) above sea level.",
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tettigonia_viridissima"
    }
  ],
  "summary": [
    "Tettigonia viridissima, the great green bush-cricket, is a large species of bush-cricket belonging to the subfamily Tettigoniinae.",
    "This species can be encountered in most of Europe, in the eastern Palearctic realm, in the Near East, and in North Africa, especially in meadows, grasslands, prairies and occasionally in gardens at an elevation up to 1,800 metres (5,900 ft) above sea level."
  ],
